WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:13.420
The next component under mode handlers and system messages is the bottom sheet. It exists on mobile only, not on desktop, and usually not on tablets either.

2
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:23.480
It looks like this: a window too, but stuck to the bottom of the screen, anchored there. It slides up from the bottom and slides back down.

3
00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:33.480
Sometimes it shows details about what I'm looking at, or gives me other actions I can take. It's a common component; most of us have surely used it.

4
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:46.180
Its types, as you'd expect: the modal bottom sheet isolates the background, so as a user I can't interact with the content behind it; I use only the sheet, or close it and go back.

5
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.180
As usual, a scrim can go with it: the slightly transparent layer that separates the content in the background.

6
00:00:51.780 --> 00:01:04.560
The second type, as you'd expect, is the non-modal bottom sheet, like in map apps: I can still use the map itself, and there's a bottom sheet with actions and other data,

7
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:17.200
about something I selected in the product. So I can use the bottom sheet and the rest of the page content; I'm not cut off from it.

8
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:31.180
Some bottom sheets are expandable: they start as a strip at the bottom showing the title of what I'm listening to, and tapping it expands it into a full bottom sheet with more actions

9
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:40.500
and more details about the same thing. It can even expand to full screen as a whole page, and I can go back from it to the previous state.

10
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:50.640
Now some guidelines for bottom sheets. In the example in front of us, if we look for the problem, we'll notice the bottom sheet is rather large,

11
00:01:50.640 --> 00:02:01.380
reaching almost to the top of the phone. So if I want to close it and pull it down, I may pull down the phone's notification drawer or control panel instead,

12
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:10.660
which comes down like this, and the bottom sheet stays open. So I get a bit confused: I wanted to close it, and the notification bar came down.

13
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:24.100
Here I need another way to close it besides dragging the handle down. So I can add a close button with an X that lets me close it easily without dragging,

14
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.320
especially when the bottom sheet is large, like this example.

15
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:39.060
Another common mistake: using a bottom sheet for a whole flow, like pages one after another or a full process. It isn't made for that; the user can tap outside at any moment and close it by mistake,

16
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:51.160
or tap the close button and close it. Moving between several steps in it isn't easy, and all the steps can be lost by one mistake. It isn't designed for that.

17
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:59.860
A bottom sheet is usually designed for short interactions: simple things, like viewing details, taking an action, or writing a post.

18
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:09.380
But it isn't designed for going through a flow inside it, like viewing a product's details and then checking out. It doesn't fit that,

19
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:14.780
nor, say, a calendar where I pick a date and a time and go on through a process. That isn't a good use of bottom sheets.

